 ],
 "literal": "House-dwelling fowl's short-legged hen / as the wildcat of the fence approached in the evening / not knowing the place to enter, having gathered together / like calling its distressed brood of chicks / with the disagreeably sounding gossip / do not come, may you live long, O lord, on our street!",
 "translation": "May you live long, O lord! Do not come to our street accompanied by this painful gossip, which sounds like a short-legged domestic hen calling out to her distressed brood of chicks, huddled together in fear, not knowing where to hide when the wildcat from the fence approaches in the evening.",
 "modern": "வேலியிலிருந்து காட்டுப்பூனை மாலை நேரத்தில் நெருங்கியதால், புகலிடம் அறியாமல் ஒன்றுசேர்ந்து கூடியிருக்கும் தன் துன்பப்படும் குஞ்சுகளின் கூட்டத்தைக் கூப்பிடும் வீட்டுக்கோழியின் குட்டைக் கால்களைக் கொண்ட பேடையைப் போல, துன்பம் தரும் அலர் பேசும் இந்தத் தெருவில், ஐயனே! நீர் வர வேண்டாம்; நீர் வாழ்வீராக!",
 "explanation": "The speaker is the heroine's friend addressing the hero who has been visiting the heroine secretly at night. She compares the loud, distressing gossip of the village to the frantic cries of a short-legged domestic hen. In the evening, when a wildcat from the fence approaches, the hen and her chicks do not know where to hide, so they huddle together and cry out in fear. Similarly, the village women have gathered and are spreading painful rumors about the secret meetings of the lovers. The friend politely but firmly asks the hero to stop his secret night visits to their street and instead seek a formal marriage to protect the heroine's honor. This poem belongs to the Marutham landscape, where domestic scenes and village gossip are prominent themes.",
